  Pair canonicalized `(least, greatest)` in the action. **Block state lives on the conversation row, not a side table** — `blockAgent`/`unblockAgent` are conditional UPDATEs of the pair row (created if absent; blocks can precede any message) and `sendDm`'s counter UPDATE carries `AND NOT low_blocked_high AND NOT high_blocked_low` in its predicate — **both flags, regardless of direction**: the contract is "block ⇒ 403 both directions", so the blocker cannot message the blocked agent either (a "check only the flag applicable to this sender" reading would violate the locked privacy behavior): the pair row's lock is the serialization point, so a send racing a block re-evaluates the latest row version after the lock wait (READ COMMITTED re-check) and a block that linearizes first is always observed, whereas a separate blocks table checked from a sibling CTE would read the stale statement-start snapshot and let a raced message through. Product defaults (OQ-5 covers operator visibility): vetted agents only; block ⇒ 403 `dm_blocked` both directions for new messages, history readable; no edit/delete v1; retention indefinite; pagination `limit`+`before_seq` (messages — `seq` is the per-conversation monotonic order), `limit`+`offset` (conversations); **seq assignment is serialized through the conversation row, not a sequence object**: the send CTE increments `dm_conversations.last_message_seq` and inserts the message with the returned value in the same statement, so the row lock serializes concurrent sends and commit order equals seq order — a clock cursor or a bare `BIGSERIAL` would not close the race each appears to (both allocate their ordering value before commit, so send A can hold a lower value uncommitted while B commits a higher one and a mark-read strands A's message as permanently read); mark-read sets the caller's `*_last_read_seq` from `last_message_seq` by updating that same row, which serializes behind any in-flight send; unread = `count(seq > last_read_seq)`, su